course: biology
lesson: 218
title: Bees and wasps in the garden
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On a sunny day a honeybee lands on a flower, drinks sweet nectar with its long tongue and flies off with yellow balls of pollen packed on its back legs, like a shopper with two full bags.
explain: Back in the hive it does a little waggle dance, and the direction and length of the dance tell the other bees exactly where the flowers are.
explain: A honeybee hive is one big family: one queen lays the eggs, thousands of worker sisters do the work, and the nectar they bring is turned into honey, food for the winter.
explain: Most kinds of bee are not like that at all: they live alone, in holes in walls and tunnels in the ground, and they almost never sting.
explain: Wasps that pester us at picnics in late summer are not villains either: all summer they hunt caterpillars and flies in the garden, and they carry pollen from flower to flower too.
fact: Bees collect nectar and pollen from flowers; nectar is turned into honey and pollen feeds the young bees.
fact: A honeybee does a waggle dance in the hive; the angle and length of the dance tell other bees which way and how far to fly for food.
fact: Bumblebees can fly on cold days because they shiver their flight muscles to warm up, and their furry coats keep the heat in.
fact: A honeybee worker that stings a person usually dies, because its barbed sting stays in our skin; bumblebees and wasps can sting again and again.
fact: Beekeepers wear a suit and veil and puff smoke at the hive; smoke hides the bees' alarm smell and makes them eat honey, so they stay calm.
fact: Dandelions and other early flowers are important food for bees in spring, when few other flowers are open.
fact: Most kinds of bee live alone; a solitary bee lays its eggs in a hole in a wall, a hollow stem or a tunnel in the ground.
fact: A honeybee queen can lay up to about two thousand eggs a day, and the worker bees feed and care for the young.
fact: Bees have disappeared from some places because of pesticides (sprays that kill insects), illness and the loss of flowers.
fact: Wasps like sweet food in late summer, but they also eat many garden pests such as caterpillars and flies, and some help pollinate flowers.
fact: If a wasp comes to your drink, stay calm, cover the drink and move away slowly, because swatting and waving can make it sting.
fact: Honey hardly ever goes off, because it is so sugary and dry that germs cannot grow in it.
fact: Like every insect, a bee or a wasp has six legs, two antennae and two pairs of wings.
fact: All the worker bees in a honeybee hive are females; the males, called drones, do not collect food and have no sting.
exercise: What do bees collect from flowers? | Nectar and pollen | Seeds and leaves | Petals and stems | Roots and soil
exercise: What happens to the nectar honeybees bring home? | It is turned into honey | It is fed only to the drones | It is used to build the wax | It is spat out of the hive
exercise: What does a honeybee's waggle dance tell the other bees? | Which way and how far to fly for food | That it is time for all the bees to sleep | That the queen has died | Which bees are the best at dancing in the hive
exercise: How can a bumblebee fly on a cold morning? | It shivers its flight muscles to warm up | It drinks warm nectar kept in the hive | It waits for the sun to dry its wings | It flies faster to stop freezing
exercise: Why does a honeybee usually die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting stays stuck in our skin | It runs out of poison and starves | It is too tired to fly home | The other bees throw it out of the hive
exercise: Which of these can sting more than once? | A wasp | A honeybee worker | A honeybee drone | A butterfly
exercise: Why does a beekeeper puff smoke at the hive? | It hides the alarm smell and makes the bees eat honey | It puts the bees to sleep for the whole of the winter | It chases the queen out of the hive | It kills the germs on the honey so that it keeps for longer
exercise: Why is a lawn full of dandelions good for bees in spring? | Dandelions are early flowers full of food | Bees nest inside the dandelion clocks | Dandelions keep wasps away from bees | Bees eat the dandelion leaves
exercise: How do most kinds of bee live? | Alone, nesting in holes and tunnels | In huge hives with a queen in charge | In nests hanging from trees | In beekeepers' wooden boxes in the garden
exercise: What is the queen honeybee's main job? | Laying eggs, up to about two thousand a day | Giving orders to the workers each morning | Guarding the door of the hive | Making all the honey herself
exercise: Who feeds and cares for the young bees in a hive? | The worker bees | The queen on her own | The drones, the male bees | The beekeeper, by hand
exercise: Why have bees disappeared from some places? | Pesticides, illness and loss of flowers | Too many people eating honey on toast | Bees moved to live in the sea | Bees got tired of making honey and stopped
exercise: Why do wasps buzz around picnics in late summer? | They want sweet food | They want to sting everyone | They are lost and looking for home | They love the smell of plastic plates
exercise: How do wasps help the garden? | They eat pests like caterpillars and flies | They dig holes for the plants' roots | They make honey for the birds in winter | They keep the grass short
exercise: Why does honey stay good for such a long time? | It is so sugary and dry that germs cannot grow | Bees put salt in it to keep it fresh | Honey is made of wax | It is kept cold inside the hive all year round, like in a fridge
exercise: Are the worker bees in a honeybee hive male or female? | All female | All of them male | Half and half | Neither, they have no sex
test: «there's 5 bees on the lavender, how many legs is that» | 30 | 20 | 40 | 25
test: In a cartoon film a male bee goes out every day to collect nectar and make honey. What is true? | Real worker bees that collect nectar are all females | Real male bees do all of the work in the hive | Real bees have no males at all | Real male bees grow up to become the queens
test: «my brother says all bees die if they sting u, so they never sting» What is right? | Only a honeybee worker usually dies; bumblebees can sting again | All bees die after one sting | No bee has a sting at all, they only bite | Only bumblebees die after stinging; honeybees can sting again and again
test: What would happen to bees in spring if every dandelion and early flower were cut down? | They would have much less food when they need it | They would make more honey than ever before | Nothing, because bees never visit early flowers in spring | They would eat the grass instead
test: «there's a jar of honey at the back of the cupboard from like 5 yrs ago, is it gone off??» What is most likely? | It is probably still good, because germs cannot grow in honey | It is poisonous after one year, so throw it away | It has turned into wax | It has turned into vinegar, because all sweet things go sour in the end
test: «wasp keeps coming at my lemonade, piss off!!» What is the best thing to do? | Stay calm, cover the drink and move away slowly | Swat at it as hard as you can with a book | Shout and wave your arms at it | Hold the cup up near your face so it cannot get in
test: Count them: 3 bumblebees and 2 wasps sit on a fence. How many wings altogether? | 20 | 10 | 12 | 30
test: A tiny bee goes into a hole in a garden wall and never meets any other bee. What kind is it most likely? | A solitary bee laying its eggs | A honeybee that got lost | A queen honeybee in hiding | A bee that is too ill to fly
test: «bee's doing a weird wiggly dance on the comb lol» What is it doing? | Telling the others where the flowers are | Getting rid of fleas | Trying to scare the queen out of the hive | Warming up before it goes to sleep for the night
test: What would happen if a farmer sprayed a field of flowers with a strong bug poison? | Bees visiting the flowers could get ill and die | Bees would make more honey | Bees would grow bigger stings to fight back | Nothing, because bug poison only hurts the bugs that eat crops
test: A beekeeper opens a hive in a suit and puffs smoke. Why does she not get stung all over? | The smoke hides the alarm smell and the suit protects her | Bees cannot see people dressed in white | Bees only sting at night when it is dark | The smoke makes the bees go blind for an hour, so they cannot find her
exam: Identify the principal function of the waggle dance performed by the honeybee within the hive. | The communication of the direction and distance of a source of food | The selection of a new queen from among the workers | The removal of parasites from the body of the dancer by vigorous motion | The generation of heat during the winter months
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of stinging for the honeybee worker? | The barbed sting remains lodged in the skin and the insect usually perishes | The insect invariably survives and stings repeatedly thereafter | The insect is subsequently expelled from the colony by the queen as a punishment | The sting is regrown within a period of one day
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the domestic arrangements of the majority of bee species. | The majority live alone and lay eggs in holes and tunnels | The majority live in large colonies governed by a single queen | The majority inhabit wooden hives provided by beekeepers | The majority construct suspended nests of paper
exam: Ascertain the reason for which honey remains fit for consumption for an exceptionally long period. | Its high sugar content and low moisture prevent the growth of microorganisms | It is preserved by salt added by the worker bees | It is protected by a layer of wax throughout the entire period of its use | It contains a poison which is fatal to all insects and to every microorganism
